],"podcasts":[],"lists":[{"id":186303735515973,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1981","rank":100,"scaruffi":null},{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","rank":100,"scaruffi":null}],"n_lists":2}},{"id":72882034152295,"type":"song","name":"In the End","artist":"Andy Gibb","year":null,"weight":7,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[{"name":"For the Record - The 70s","n":2}],"lists":[],"n_lists":0}},{"id":191989701156764,"type":"song","name":"In the End (NA)","artist":"Andy Gibb","year":null,"weight":4,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[{"name":"For the Record - The 70s","n":1}],"lists":[],"n_lists":0}}],"members":[{"id":230581048359579,"type":"artist","name":"Joe Walsh (guitar on specific tracks)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":95570719038486,"type":"artist","name":"Barry Gibb (backup vocals, co-writer)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"member_of":[{"id":226256774569652,"type":"artist","name":"The Bee Gees","artist":null,"year":1958,"weight":325,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":167075640007578,"type":"artist","name":"The Bee Gees (expressed interest to join)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":144987903140772,"type":"artist","name":"Bee Gees (brother of members)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":114785881376127,"type":"artist","name":"Bee Gees (siblings)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"genres":[{"id":157581530936183,"type":"genre","name":"Light Disco","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":52056820540771,"type":"genre","name":"Plush Pop","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":136113600818764,"type":"genre","name":"Yacht Rock","n":1}],"labels":[{"id":154033766396652,"type":"label","name":"RCA Records","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":585,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Personal Milestones Concurrent with Success (1978)","body":"Just weeks after his song \"Love Is Thicker Than Water\" hit number one on his last day as a teenager (March 4, 1978), Andy Gibb became a father and then divorced, marking a tumultuous period in his personal life. \"In a few weeks, Andy would be a daddy. In a few weeks after that, he would be divorced.\"","year":1978,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-34-a-70s-pop-manifesto-finding-meaning-in-70s-pop"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Alleged Chart Manipulation by RCA","body":"There is a legend that Andy Gibb's record label, RCA, allegedly threatened not to allow him to participate in a Billboard show if \"Shadow Dancing\" was not kept at number 1. This alleged interference is cited as the reason why Gerry Rafferty's \"Baker Street\" remained at number 2 on the Billboard Hot 100 for six weeks. \"RCA, Andy Gibb's record label allegedly threatened to not let him take part in some sort of billboard show If shadow dancing was not still number 1\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 38 - The Influence of Jazz on 70s Popular Music","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 38 - The Influence of Jazz on 70s Popular Music","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-38-the-influence-of-jazz-on-70s-popular-music"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Desire to Be the Fourth Bee Gee","body":"Andy Gibb at one point expressed a willingness to give up his solo career to become the fourth member of The Bee Gees, although the sincerity of this statement was questioned. \"I'm not sure if Andy meant it when he said he was willing to give up his solo career to be the fourth Bee Gee or not.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-34-a-70s-pop-manifesto-finding-meaning-in-70s-pop"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Family Connection to The Bee Gees","body":"Andy Gibb benefited from being the brother of the Bee Gees, who were \"Billboard top 40 giants\" and dominated the charts, giving him a significant advantage in the music industry. He also bore a strong resemblance to a young Barry Gibb. \"if you were going to have brothers in the music industry, I don't think you could have done any better than being the brother of the Bee Gees.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-34-a-70s-pop-manifesto-finding-meaning-in-70s-pop"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Later Career Challenges and Independence (Early 1980s)","body":"By the early 1980s, Andy Gibb was no longer a teen idol and struggled to sell records. He also dated Victoria Principal, a star from the popular TV show \"Dallas.\" During this period, he voiced a desire for independence from his brothers' influence, stating he wanted to \"do his own thing\" and no longer wanted Barry to produce his records. \"But for a while it lasted, he was one of the biggest stars in the music world. He said that he and his brother made music that was happy.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-34-a-70s-pop-manifesto-finding-meaning-in-70s-pop"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Only Artist with Back-to-Back Songs of Summer","body":"Andy Gibb holds the distinction, according to Billboard, as the only artist to have won the Song of the Summer title two years in a row, with \"I Just Want to Be Your Everything\" in 1977 and \"Shadow Dancing\" in 1978. \"According to Billboard, he currently holds the distinction as the only artist to have the song of the summer two years in a row.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Song(s) of the Summer Edition Part 2","url":null},{"source":"Hit Parade Music History and Music Trivia","episode":"Song(s) of the Summer Edition Part 2","url":"https://shows.acast.com/hit-parade/episodes/695d4ef50c30a1408dc292e4"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Recording with Joe Walsh","body":"While recording his debut album \"Flowing Rivers,\" Andy Gibb had Joe Walsh of The Eagles play guitar on two of his hit songs, \"I Just Want to Be Your Everything\" and \"Love Is Thicker Than Water.\" \"Joe Walsh is willing to play on I just want to be your everything and love is thicker than water. You don't say no, right?\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 34 - A 70s Pop Manifesto - Finding Meaning in 70s Pop","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-34-a-70s-pop-manifesto-finding-meaning-in-70s-pop"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best New Artist (1978)","body":"Nominated.","year":1978,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1453643"}]}],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":107036981264816,"type":"song","name":"I Just Want to Be